The circuit is an active shunt–shunt feedback transimpedance amplifier: its open-circuit transresistance is approximately $1/g_{m2}$, while feedback makes both the input and output resistances low.

### Circuit operation

Let the input node be $X$, with small-signal voltage $v_x$, and call the unlabeled bottom current sink $I_B$.

- $M_1$ is an NMOS common-gate device: its source is at $X$, its drain is at the output, and its gate is fixed at $V_b$.
- $M_2$ is a PMOS feedback device: its source is at $V_{DD}$, its gate senses $V_{\text{out}}$, and its drain returns current to $X$.
- $I_1$ biases $M_1$ and acts as its drain load.

With no DC load at the output,

$$I_{D1}=I_1,\qquad I_{D2}=I_B-I_1-I_{\text{in,DC}}.$$

Also,

$$V_X=V_b-V_{GS1},\qquad V_{\text{out}}=V_{DD}-V_{SG2}.$$

Saturation requires $V_{\text{out}}-V_X\ge V_{\text{ov1}}$ and $V_{DD}-V_X\ge V_{\text{ov2}}$, together with sufficient headroom for the current sources.

The feedback is negative: an increase in $I_{\text{in}}$ initially raises $V_X$, reduces $M_1$'s $V_{GS}$, raises $V_{\text{out}}$, lowers $M_2$'s $V_{SG}$, and therefore reduces the current supplied by $M_2$ to node $X$.

### Small-signal analysis

Define

$$G_1=g_{m1}+g_{mb1},$$

where $g_{mb1}=0$ if body effect is neglected. Since $M_2$'s source and body are both at $V_{DD}$, it has no body effect. Taking $i_{d2}$ positive from source to drain,

$$i_{d1}=-G_1v_x+\frac{v_{\text{out}}-v_x}{r_{o1}},$$

$$i_{d2}=-g_{m2}v_{\text{out}}-\frac{v_x}{r_{o2}}.$$

For an ideal $I_1$ and an unloaded output, output-node KCL gives $i_{d1}=0$, so

$$\frac{v_{\text{out}}}{v_x}=1+G_1r_{o1}\equiv D\approx G_1r_{o1}.$$

Input-node KCL gives $i_{\text{in}}=g_{m2}v_{\text{out}}+v_x/r_{o2}$. Hence the transresistance is

$$\boxed{
R_m\equiv\frac{v_{\text{out}}}{i_{\text{in}}}
=\frac{Dr_{o2}}{1+g_{m2}r_{o2}D}
\approx\frac{1}{g_{m2}}
}.$$

The resistance looking into node $X$ is

$$\boxed{
R_{\text{in}}=\frac{v_x}{i_{\text{in}}}
=\frac{r_{o2}}{1+g_{m2}r_{o2}D}
\approx\frac{1}{g_{m2}G_1r_{o1}}
}.$$

Thus the input behaves like a low-impedance, approximately virtual-ground node, although the finite error voltage $v_x=v_{\text{out}}/D$ remains.

The core output resistance, excluding the finite impedance of $I_1$, is

$$R_{\text{out,core}}
=\frac{G_1+g_{ds1}+g_{ds2}}
{g_{m2}(G_1+g_{ds1})+g_{ds1}g_{ds2}}
\approx\boxed{\frac{1}{g_{m2}}}.$$

A finite current-source resistance $r_{I1}$ appears in parallel with this value.

Intuitively, almost all of the signal input current is absorbed by the change in $M_2$'s current, $i_{d2}\approx-i_{\text{in}}$, while $M_1$'s current remains nearly constant. Therefore $v_{\text{out}}\approx i_{\text{in}}/g_{m2}}$, with $M_1$ serving as the high-gain error amplifier that keeps the input-node movement small.
